              "title": "MHT CET 2024 (16 May Shift 1)"
            }
          ],
          "question": {
            "text": "A simple pendulum has a periodic time ' $\\mathrm{T}_1$ ' when it is on the surface of earth of radius ' $R$ '. Its periodic time is ' $T_2$ ' when it is taken to a height ' $R$ ' above the earth's surface. The value of $\\frac{T_2}{T_1}$ is",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "jGtTA",
              "text": "$\\sqrt{2}$",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "rSFMM",
              "text": "1",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "zQvIA",
              "text": "2",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": true
            },
            {
              "id": "l9rOi",
              "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}$",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "The periodic time of a simple pendulum is given by $T = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g}}$.<br><br>On the surface of the earth, the acceleration due to gravity is $g_1 = \\dfrac{GM}{R^2}$ and the periodic time is $T_1 = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g_1}}$.<br><br>At a height $h = R$ above the earth's surface, the distance from the center of the earth is $r = R + h = 2R$.<br><br>The acceleration due to gravity at this height is $g_2 = \\dfrac{GM}{(2R)^2} = \\dfrac{g_1}{4}$.<br><br>The periodic time at this height is $T_2 = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g_2}}$.<br><br>Taking the ratio of the periodic times:<br><br>$\\dfrac{T_2}{T_1} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_1}{g_2}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{g_1}{g_1 / 4}} = \\sqrt{4} = 2$.<br><br>Answer: 2",
            "image": null
          },
          "videoSolution": null,
          "syllabusCategory": "noChange",
          "questionStructure": [
            "default"
          ],
