            }
          ],
          "question": {
            "text": "A meter scale is supported on a wedge at its centre of gravity. A body of weight ' $w$ ' is suspended from the 20 cm mark and another weight of 25 gram is suspended from 74 cm mark balances it and the meter scale remains perfectly horizontal. Neglecting the weight of the meter scale, the weight of the body is",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "r6Klj",
              "text": "33 gram wt.",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "Mzpb5",
              "text": "30 gram wt.",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "gU29K",
              "text": "20 gram wt.",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": true
            },
            {
              "id": "kKgsQ",
              "text": "15 gram wt.",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "The meter scale is supported at its centre of gravity, which is at the $50\\text{ cm}$ mark.<br><br>The distance of the weight $w$ from the pivot is $50 - 20 = 30\\text{ cm}$.<br><br>The distance of the $25\\text{ g}$ weight from the pivot is $74 - 50 = 24\\text{ cm}$.<br><br>For the meter scale to remain perfectly horizontal, the net torque about the pivot must be zero. Equating the anticlockwise torque to the clockwise torque:<br><br>$w \\times 30 = 25 \\times 24$<br><br>$w = \\dfrac{25 \\times 24}{30}$<br><br>$w = 20\\text{ gram wt.}$<br><br>Answer: 20 gram wt.",
            "image": null
          },
          "videoSolution": null,
          "syllabusCategory": "noChange",
          "questionStructure": [
            "default"
          ],
          "previousYear": {
